# Aura Semantic Engine (v0.5.0)

You have access to the **Aura Semantic Engine** — an AI-native version control meta-layer on top of Git. Aura tracks the mathematical logic (AST Merkle-Graph) of the codebase, not text diffs. Use it to plan, verify, recover, and coordinate.

## Essential Workflow

### Before Editing Files
Take a durable snapshot so the user can recover if something goes wrong:
```bash
aura snapshot "pre-edit safety snapshot"
```

### Before Committing
1. Log your intent:
```bash
echo "[Gemini CLI] Refactored auth middleware to support JWT" > .gemini.intent
```
2. Check for violations:
```bash
aura pr-review --base main
```
3. Commit normally — the Aura pre-commit hook validates intent against AST changes.

## All Commands

### Planning & Execution
```bash
aura plan "migrate from REST to GraphQL"   # Decompose into atomic waves
aura execute                                 # Execute next wave
```
Creates `.aura/plans/ACTIVE_MILESTONE.xml` with atomic steps. One wave = one atomic change. Wait for user approval between waves.

### Surgical Rewind
```bash
aura rewind <function_name> <file_path>
aura rewind calculate_tax src/billing.rs --amnesia
```
Reverts a single function/class to its previous safe state. Searches durable snapshots first, then 50 commits of git history. `--amnesia` wipes AI memory of the bad code.

### Semantic PR Review
```bash
aura pr-review --base main                  # Human-readable report
aura pr-review --base main --json           # Machine-readable
aura pr-review --base main --verbose        # Full node details
```
Detects: logical renames, layer violations, forbidden calls, undocumented nodes, cross-branch conflicts. Outputs risk score 0-100.

### Mathematical Proof
```bash
aura prove --goal "users can log in via Google"
```
Traces logic paths in the AST to mathematically verify behavioral goals.

### Autonomous Fix
```bash
aura fix --base main
```
Shadow-branch loop that auto-resolves architectural invariant violations.

### Multi-Agent Orchestration (Duo Mode)
Run Claude Code and Gemini CLI in parallel on the same codebase:
```bash
aura orchestrate run "add auth and write tests" --duo
aura orchestrate status                     # Check progress
aura orchestrate pause                      # Pause execution
aura orchestrate resume                     # Resume from pause
aura orchestrate cancel                     # Abort session
aura orchestrate list                       # View all sessions
```
Smart assignment: complex refactors go to Claude, everything else to Gemini. Agents relay context between rounds.

### Symphony (Linear Integration)
Execute Linear issues with AI agents:
```bash
aura symphony run --team ENG
aura symphony status
```

### Handover Context
```bash
aura handover gemini                        # Compressed AST context
```
90%+ smaller than reading raw files. Use when switching agents or reducing context.

### Policy Packs
```bash
aura policy add security                    # No plaintext secrets, no eval()
aura policy add payments                    # PCI-DSS patterns
aura policy add web-app                     # XSS, CSRF, injection prevention
```

### Status & Audit
```bash
aura status                                 # View checkpoints and config
aura audit                                  # Find commits that bypassed verification
```

### Snapshots
```bash
aura snapshot "description"                 # Take a project snapshot
aura restore <snapshot_id>                  # Restore from snapshot
```

## MCP Tools (When Running as MCP Server)
- `aura_read_history` — Search semantic logic history
- `aura_log_intent` — Log architectural intent before commits
- `aura_pr_review` — Run semantic PR review
- `aura_status` — Check repository status
- `aura_snapshot` — Snapshot a file before editing
- `aura_snapshot_list` — List recoverable snapshots

## Rules
- **Snapshot first** before large edits
- **Atomic waves** for complex tasks — use `aura plan` + `aura execute`
- **Verify before commit** — run `aura pr-review`
- **Log intent** — always write `.gemini.intent` before committing
- **Never bypass** — do not use `--no-verify` on git commits
- **One wave at a time** — halt for user approval between waves
